Page the change log from the last consumed sequence, not the one after it

The gateway skips frames whose commitSeq is <= from. api.go says so
directly, and its README tells clients to "keep paging from the last
consumed commitSeq". The notebook asked for watermark + 1, so every call
skipped exactly one frame: the first unconsumed one.

That is silent data loss, and it hid in the worst possible way. A BENCH
on the source emitted a single RESET frame, the notebook asked for the
sequence after it, the gateway returned nothing, and the run reported
Completed having done no work at all. Three of the four paging sites had
the same fault, including the one inside the rebuild loop.

No test covered the value of `from`, which is how it shipped. Three now
do, and they fail against the old arithmetic.

A RESET now fails with an explanation rather than a bare exception.
TRUNCATE, RESTORE and BENCH replace the table wholesale and the engine
reports each as one RESET carrying no rows, by deliberate design. The
change log therefore cannot rebuild the table: replaying it clears the
image and leaves nothing. Refusing is correct, and the message now says
so, names the sequence, and states that the lakehouse table was left
untouched - because a reader who sees "reset" should not have to guess
whether their data survived.

